Turkey's ambassador to London says his  country's offensive in Syria will be a  success he also says its actions won't  be influenced by Donald Trump who used  Twitter to warn Ankara not to go off  limits  Turkey's a big country and proud country  and then we are not making our policies  according to the zig zagging to tweet  messages I mean we did not plan our  operation according to the first message  or we did not make any change according  to the second one in do you have any  idea what a limit means I don't know but  our limit is ensuring our safety  ensuring our national border safe and  saving Syrians from the Chitauri of the  terrorist organization and creating an  environment for voluntary return of  Syrians  the problem is Turkey boos Kurdish  fighters like these as terrorists  whereas Britain and the United States  see them as allies in the fight against  Islamic state such as here during the  fall of Raqqa  it set the NATO allies on a collision  course in a war zone that's already  deeply complex turkey shares a 900  kilometer border with Syria leaving it  exposed to Islamic state fighters and  the threat it sees from Kurdish militias  like the YPG Turkish forces have already  launched two operations in Syria since  2016 one in the town of Dara bliss and  another against the enclaves of Afrin  the next offensive aims to create a 30  kilometre buffer zone on the border  President Trump says Turkey will be  responsible for all captured Isis  fighters currently held in camps by the  Kurds  but ambassador Yelchin says each country  should take charge of its own Isis  members this includes Britain even  though current UK policy is to wash its  hands of them when they were leaving  their countries they had their  nationality and passports and they  became terrorists or maybe some wives or  kids are innocent I don't know but those  countries should take those people back  to their own countries and they can  bring them justice they can send them to  court or they can rehabilitate them and  having spoken specifically to Britain  about that we have talked this issue in  the past especially if that these  foreign terrorist fighters are in Turkey  they are providing consular services to  them and also according to that joint  declaration of intent  if those terrorists are in Turkey we can  deport them I think we can be creative  and we can find some new mechanisms for  cooperation these words may ring alarm  bells in Whitehall ministers are split  over what to do with anyone linked to  Isis who once held a British passport  but as the Turkish offensive ramps up  Britain may be forced to review its  policy of no return  Deborah Haynes Sky News
 

